The new conformance gate has both a false-green path for a public engine method losing its receiver and a false-red path for nested module inner attributes in pinned test files. These affect the accuracy and usability of the publish/CI gate.

Full review comments:

- [P2] Assert configure_projections remains an Engine method — /home/coreyt/projects/fathomdb-worktrees/orch-0.8.20-s30/scripts/check-c1-conformance.sh:718-721
  When this gate is used as the publish precondition, these `fn_sig` probes still pass if `configure_projections` is changed into an associated/free function with the same `specs`, `drop`, and return text but no `&self` receiver. That would break the instance verb the pin evidence records and call sites invoke as `engine.configure_projections(...)`, while the conformance job would still report green; bind this assertion to the receiver/inherent `Engine` method as well as the parameter fragments.

- [P2] Limit inner-attribute scan to the file header — /home/coreyt/projects/fathomdb-worktrees/orch-0.8.20-s30/scripts/check-c1-conformance.sh:1821-1824
  For pinned test files that add a nested module with its own legal inner attribute such as `mod helpers { #![cfg(feature = "x")] ... }`, this scans the entire file, reports the whole integration test as conditionally compiled, and blocks CI/preflight even though the top-level pinned tests still run. The check should only treat file-level inner attributes at the crate/file header (or depth 0 before items) as disabling the whole proof file.
